---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Winder, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or storm water in Winder, GA 30680, in Barrow County. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Barrow County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Winder" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can about
      the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Winder</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Winder</h2>
    <p>
      Winder is the county seat of Barrow County, not a Gwinnett suburb. It
      started out as Jug Tavern, and downtown is still around the courthouse
      square and Broad Street. Fort Yargo State Park is the backyard lake, and you
      can walk there from downtown on the Wilkins Greenway.
    </p>
    <p>
      Downtown is old. It has cottages and mill-town houses, and many sit over a
      crawl space. New builder houses are going up on the edge of town, like the
      Hills at Cedar Creek. Along Atlanta Highway and Auburn Road, toward
      Gwinnett, it is a mix of older houses, businesses, and some newer ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where you live changes what you have. Houses in town are usually on the City
      of Winder's water and sewer. Outside the city, houses are more often on a
      well and septic. A crawl space is common outside town too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Houses from the late 1970s to the mid-1990s that were remodeled may have
      gray plastic pipe, called polybutylene, which is known to fail. The new
      houses do not. You cannot tell from the neighborhood, so ask a plumber if
      you are not sure.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. Tell us if the house has a crawl space, a basement,
      or a slab, and whether you are on city water or a well.
    </p>
    <p>
      Winder is outside Gwinnett, where this site started. The companies that
      answer will tell you if they can come to your address. If they cannot, we do
      not pretend they can.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage. During Hurricane Helene in 2024, Barrow County had
      about 6 to 7 inches of rain, and trees and power lines came down in the
      area. A damaged roof can leak into the attic for days before the stain shows
      up on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>Winder has one ZIP code, 30680.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Downtown and the courthouse square.</strong> Historic and
      mid-century cottages and mill-town houses. Crawl spaces are common.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Fort Yargo edge.</strong> Older houses near the park, for people who
      bought for the lake.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Atlanta Highway and Auburn Road.</strong> Older houses, businesses,
      and some new ones. Commuters toward Gwinnett live here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Hills at Cedar Creek.</strong> New builder houses from the
      mid-2020s, on city sewer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Outside the city.</strong> Older houses on wells and septic, often
      over a crawl space.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Winder</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Slow leak in a crawl space.</strong> A supply line or drain drips for
        weeks. The insulation hangs down wet and the floor above gets soft.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gray plastic pipe.</strong> Some remodeled houses from the late 1970s
        to the mid-1990s may have polybutylene pipe that is known to fail.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett and Walton had many in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm damage.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a door, or a
        roof damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Septic backup.</strong> Outside the city, wastewater can back up into
        a tub or floor drain. This is sewage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Winder homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>Will someone come out to Winder?</dt>
        <dd>
          It depends on the company and on where in Winder you are. The companies
          that answer will tell you if they can come to your address. Give your
          street and ZIP, and say if you are in town or outside it. If no one can
          come, we will say so, and you can call a local Barrow County company.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Water is coming up near my meter or in the street. Who do I call?</dt>
        <dd>
          In Winder, call the City of Winder Water Department. It is not the
          Gwinnett County water department. If you are outside the city and on a
          well, the problem is yours or your well company's. If water is inside
          your house, shut off the main valve, take photos, and call us about the
          wet part inside.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          My house is outside the city, on a well and septic. Does that change
          anything?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Yes. A septic backup is sewage, and it needs a septic company for the
          cause and cleanup for the inside. A well pump or pressure tank that leaks
          is a well company's job, and drying the house is ours. Tell us which you
          have when you call.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          I have an older house downtown with plaster walls and wood floors. Does
          that change how it is dried?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          It can. Old plaster and hardwood take water differently from new drywall
          and vinyl. Tell us the age of the house and what the walls and floors are
          made of, and do not tear anything out yourself. Take photos, and call.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer or septic backup. Say which one
      it is on the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="winder" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/dacula">Dacula</a> ·
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/grayson">Grayson</a> ·
      <a href="/loganville">Loganville</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
